are true Catholic churches under the Pope in Rome (they're different
from Eastern Orthodox churches).  Historically, that was the norm for
more than half of the Church's history.

Even in the "normal western" church (the Latinate Catholic church) it's
not unheard of.  When I was growing up, our priest was married with
children.
There are a number of circumstances under which it occurs.  In the US
and England, conversion of married Episcopalian priests is the most
common, but even very conservative areas ordain married priests from
time to time (there was such a case in Spain in August this year,
approved by the Pope).

